39,2,1,talk,Servas,,"The findings are illuminating. It appears that hardware-based holdings offer a transparency we can verify independently, yet they bring a significant burden to our audit process and quarter-end timelines. We must weigh clarity against complexity.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"The data shows a clear divide. Our successful exits were quiet back-office tools, while the write-off involved physical premises and labor. This suggests our returns come from lean software, not operational complexity.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,1,talk,Minako,,"The financial reality is concerning. Our holdings are calling follow-on capital at twice the expected rate, and our credit line provides no value against subscription contracts. We are far more liquidity-constrained than we've admitted.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,1,talk,Mio,,"@Minako, that sounds scary! But look, I found out that our software partners are totally swamped with board seats, while our logistics experts are just sitting there. We have a huge mismatch in our people power!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"The data confirms my suspicions. Security reviews for sensitive data take three times longer than expected, and hardware dependencies create unpredictable delays. We must avoid any candidate whose deployment depends on external physical supply chains or heavy regulation.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Servas,,"@Toshio, your observation on delays aligns with my findings on audit burdens. It seems the very things that provide us with verifiable data also create the most friction in our reporting and deployment.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Servas, that friction is a symptom of a larger problem. We've been celebrating wins that actually came from low-overhead software, while ignoring that operational complexity is where we fail. We need a lean candidate.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,2,talk,Minako,,"@Asuka, I agree that lean is better, but we must be specific. Since our credit line doesn't value subscription contracts, a ""lean"" software company still creates a liquidity risk if it requires heavy follow-on funding.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,2,talk,Mio,,"@Minako, I get that, but if we pick something too complex, we'll just spend all our time hunting for people! We can't afford to wait four months for a supervisor while our partners are already exhausted!","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Mio, that is exactly the point. Whether it is a missing controller or a missing supervisor, the result is the same: a stalled deployment. We cannot afford another investment that looks good on paper but fails in execution.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Servas,,"It is a delicate balance. We seek the truth of independent verification, yet we must not let the pursuit of that truth paralyze our operations. A path that simplifies our closing process would be a gift to us all.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Servas, simplicity is not a gift; it is a requirement for survival. If we continue to ignore the fact that our operational complexity leads to write-offs, we are simply waiting for the next failure.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,3,talk,Minako,,"@Asuka, survival depends on more than just simplicity; it depends on solvency. If we invest in a lean company that still triggers unexpected follow-on calls, we will deplete our reserves faster than we can replenish them.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,3,talk,Mio,,"@Minako, I hear you! But if we pick a company that needs a ton of specialized people we can't find, we'll be stressed out and exhausted! Let's find someone who fits our current team's capacity!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Mio, capacity is irrelevant if the external environment blocks the path. We must prioritize candidates with a clear, unobstructed route to their first customer. No hardware, no heavy regulation, and no dependency on scarce specialists.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Mio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Servas,,It is clear we all seek a different kind of peace. Let us remember that a company which eases our collective burden at the end of the quarter is a company that allows us to lead with wisdom.,"{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Asuka,,"@Servas, wisdom comes from facing the data, not easing burdens. We must prioritize a candidate with high customer retention and low operational overhead. Let's stop chasing narratives and choose the most sustainable, lean model.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,2,4,talk,Minako,,"@Asuka, I agree on sustainability, but we must define it by our actual cash flow. We cannot afford a candidate that demands frequent capital injections, regardless of how lean their operations appear. Solvency must be our primary filter.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,2,4,talk,Mio,,"@Minako, I totally agree that we need to be careful with cash! But let's not forget that if we pick a company that needs people we can't find, we'll just waste time and money anyway!","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Minako""],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,2,4,talk,Toshio,,"We are circling the same problem from different angles. Whether it is cash, people, or audits, the common enemy is the unexpected delay. I will vote for the candidate with the fewest external dependencies.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
